{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794437850,"nonce":"hVwMm2rsyvS+RaQijdcfASc7BVg=","title":"The per-frame style pass costs 9 ms on an idle 4,000-node tree, and a 2,000-row list scrolls at 9 fps on an iPhone","message":"Found by the GPU-deferral baseline (07f1e4b).\n\nDesktop, M1 Pro, a scroll view of 2,000 rows (4,007 nodes), nothing animating: an idle `Headless` frame takes 9.3 ms and 9.1 ms of it is `RenderTree::apply_stylesheet_state_styles` (the hover and focus pass). The other steps of the frame (layout 1.5, flip bounds 0.07, the animation and transition props, focus sync) are under 1.5 ms. With the pointer over the list the frame is about 19 ms.\n\niPhone 12 Pro Max, mobile/example, scene `list` with scripted touches: 9.3 fps, 44 ms in `blinc_build_frame`, 3.4 ms in render, 9 ms on the GPU. The phone's build step is CPU-bound where the GPU is not. That the phone's 44 ms is the same pass is not confirmed; the ratio to the desktop figure (2.3x) fits a slower core and a size-optimised build.\n\nThe desktop runner gates this pass by router state (the comment in its frame code says it is skipped while the router and tree are unchanged); `Headless::frame` does not, and the web runner calls it on every frame when a stylesheet exists (read from the code, not measured). Whether the iOS runner gates it is the thing to check first.\n\nTo do: confirm which steps make up the phone's 44 ms (a Time Profiler trace attached to the running app works), then gate or incrementalise the pass so an unchanged tree costs nothing. Method and tables are in the baseline comment on 07f1e4b.","files":null}]}